"""English counterpart of the Japanese initial setup guide."""
PAGES_EN = [
('Getting started', '''<h2>Initial setup</h2><ol>
<li><b>Radio → General / Radio / Audio settings → General</b><br>Enter your callsign, location, JCC/JCG and additional TX text. The callsign is shared with the main window.</li>
<li><b>Select a model in the Radio tab</b><br>The radio-specific settings below change with the model.</li>
<li><b>Check CI-V / CAT settings</b><br>You can edit the ICOM CI-V address. Use the address configured on the radio.</li>
<li><b>Select the COM port</b><br>Use the port number and device name. Yaesu uses Enhanced COM. For selected Yaesu and Kenwood models using Hamlib, also specify the CAT speed and run the connection test.</li>
<li><b>Adjust reception in Audio IN</b><br>Select the recording device carrying audio from the radio to the computer. Before an actual RTTY signal is available, use Initial 50% and Save audio level. New configurations also start at 50%; previously saved levels are preserved. When a signal is available, adjust the slider into the blue Good range on the RX meter and save. A low reading cannot distinguish no signal from a weak signal and does not necessarily mean incorrect settings. Good does not guarantee decoding: check the received text.</li>
<li><b>Confirm the radio connection</b><br>An active operating connection from the main window can be used for the Audio OUT test. Otherwise, first complete the connection test in the Radio tab. If you changed the selected radio settings after opening this window, restore settings matching the active connection, or save and reconnect.</li>
<li><b>Adjust transmission in Audio OUT</b><br>Select the playback device carrying audio from the computer to the radio. Test tone + TX PTT actually transmits RTTY audio. Start at a low TX level and adjust the slider during transmission. If ALC is available, use the on-screen meter and green guide; otherwise watch the radio ALC meter. Set the level just below where ALC begins to move. Remaining seconds appear beside the button. Press again to stop, or wait for the automatic stop after 10 seconds. Then select Save audio level.</li>
<li><b>If using external equipment</b><br>Enable External control, select a COM port separate from CAT, choose RTS / DTR, invert polarity if necessary, and set the lead time before PTT (0.1–9.9 seconds). Verify the equipment switching before transmitting.</li>
<li><b>Save settings and connect for operation</b><br>Use Save or Connect in the Radio tab to confirm settings, then connect from the main window. Closing settings ends a test connection. Check the frequency display and RX level.</li>
<li><b>Edit → Edit macros</b><br>The default template is Normal QSO. For a contest, select the contest template, Apply, then Save. Apply replaces edited macros too. Updating PSRTTY alone preserves existing macros.</li></ol>
<h3>Profiles</h3><ol><li><b>Select a profile</b><br>Initially there is Profile1. Select a profile tab at the top of Settings, then edit General, Radio and the other tabs below. Profile names allow up to 10 letters, digits, hyphens and slashes. Add profile copies the displayed profile, up to 6 profiles. The first and active profiles cannot be deleted. Deletions take effect on Save; Cancel discards them.</li>
<li><b>Switch profiles from the main window</b><br>Select the operating profile using the buttons at the top right. The selected profile is blue. Switching is blocked during transmission, connection or settings operations. Switching disconnects the current radio. If previously connected, PSRTTY attempts to connect using the new profile; if this fails, it keeps that profile selected and shows Disconnected. If already disconnected, it does not connect automatically. Check the radio button and connect from there if needed.</li></ol>
<h3>Auto CQ in contests</h3><p>Transmits F1 the selected number of times. Defaults: 10 transmissions, waiting 7 seconds after PTT release. A radio connection is required. See TX / Auto CQ.</p>'''),
('Control', '''<h2>Operating the radio</h2><p>Open <b>Control</b> beside the frequency. Only one window is opened; pressing again brings it back near the main window.</p>
<table><tr><th>Control</th><th>Action</th></tr>
<tr><td>Band buttons</td><td>Show bands supported by the selected model. Other ICOM shows all candidates. Returns to the last frequency used on that band during this session; initially uses a preset.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Frequency → Set / Enter</td><td>Enter MHz, e.g. 21.090700. The display shows 21.090.700 MHz; the input has one decimal point.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Drag the jog dial</td><td>Clockwise increases; counterclockwise decreases. Dragging near the center does not turn it.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Mouse wheel</td><td>By default, toward you increases and away decreases. Reverse wheel reverses this and is saved.</td></tr>
<tr><td>+ / − buttons</td><td>Move by the selected STEP.</td></tr></table>
<h3>STEP (default 10 Hz)</h3><p>Select 1 Hz, 10 Hz, 100 Hz, 1 kHz (1,000 Hz), or 10 kHz (10,000 Hz) per step.</p>
<h3>FILTER</h3><p>ICOM uses FIL1 / FIL2 / FIL3. Supported Yaesu models offer mode-specific receive widths through Hamlib. TS-590SG DATA/SSB adjustment preserves low cut. TS-890S/TS-990S width control is not implemented in Hamlib 4.7.2; use the radio. NB/NR read/write is supported for all listed Yaesu/Kenwood models. A dash means unavailable or not yet read.</p>
<h3>NB / NR / NOTCH</h3><p>NB switches the noise blanker; NR switches noise reduction. NOTCH selects automatic or manual notch and switches it on or off. States are read from the radio and shown by ON/OFF and color. A dash means unavailable or unsupported. Controls are disabled while disconnected, transmitting or running Auto CQ.</p>
<p>Antenna TUNE sends the operation to ICOM radios, including configurations with an external ATU. It cannot work if the radio does not support it. Sending a command alone does not count as completion; radio state changes are checked. Operation has been verified on IC-7300 / IC-7300MK2.</p>
<p>G on band buttons means GHz. The frequency display remains in MHz even on higher bands.</p>'''),
('RX / tuning', '''<h2>Pause and resume decoding</h2><p>The checkbox at the upper right of the Decode frame starts ON. OFF pauses character decoding, RX card creation and automatic capture of CALL and other fields. Audio input, spectrum, RX meter and transmission continue. Existing cards remain. Switching ON resumes from that point.</p>
<p>Cards show time, RX and text on one line; long text wraps from the text position. RX is dark green; TX uses red text and a vermilion card. Up to 30 cards are shown.</p>
<h3>Decode SQ (default 4)</h3><p>Select OFF or 1–10 in the SQ list on the frame. SQ suppresses noise decoding; the values are not dB. The setting is saved for the next launch.</p>
<table><tr><th>Setting / condition</th><th>Action</th></tr>
<tr><td>OFF</td><td>Disables SQ suppression. Start / stop bit checks remain active.</td></tr>
<tr><td>1–10</td><td>1 is weak suppression, close to OFF. Higher values increase suppression. Start with 4.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Too many garbage characters</td><td>Increase SQ. Noise through a narrow filter may require a higher setting.</td></tr>
<tr><td>A signal is present but unreadable</td><td>Check tuning and lower SQ. Excessive SQ may lose weak signals.</td></tr></table>
<p>Display gain affects only the waveform, not SQ or decoding sensitivity. SQ suppresses character decoding while audio input, spectrum and RX meter continue. It does not discard short messages such as 599 based on length.</p>
<h2>Tuning an RTTY signal</h2><p>The short gray line at the top of the waveform marks the center between MARK and SPACE. Center below shows their average frequency and follows receive-position and shift changes.</p>
<table><tr><th>Control</th><th>Action</th></tr>
<tr><td>Click the spectrum</td><td>Moves MARK to that position and SPACE by the same amount.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Drag MARK / Center / SPACE</td><td>Moves both tones together, preserving their separation. Grab Center near the short gray line at the top.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Enter Shift</td><td>Keeps MARK fixed and moves SPACE. AUTO TUNE uses this shift.</td></tr>
<tr><td>AUTO TRACK</td><td>Initially OFF. Cautiously follows small audio-frequency drifts of the station being received. Avoids frequently jumping to other stations; pauses during manual adjustment or TX.</td></tr>
<tr><td>AUTO TUNE</td><td>Searches once for two peaks within the tolerance of the configured shift. The shift may change slightly to match the peaks. Noise can also trigger it: check the result.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Reset shift</td><td>Restores 170 Hz without moving MARK.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Reset position</td><td>Restores MARK to 2125 Hz while preserving the shift.</td></tr></table>
<p>Standard tones are MARK 2125 Hz and SPACE 2295 Hz. These controls change the audio decoding position. Use Control to change the radio frequency.</p>
<h3>Sub decoder</h3><p>Open Sub decoder to the right of Cross scope or from View. It starts closed. Read the contest-category and recommended-PC notice, then press OK. Red A and blue B search nearby signals for reception only. Both auto sweeps start ON. Dragging an A/B label or its pair of lines disables only that side's sweep. They can tune close together. If a sub decoder receives the main station, it resumes searching. Main reception takes priority; high PC load reduces sub searches and display updates. Signals outside the radio receive filter cannot be decoded.</p>
<h3>C tuning (receive-frequency offset)</h3><p>Shown beside Sub decoder at the top of the main window. It is the average offset in Hz between received MARK/SPACE and the configured positions. <b>Positive means the signal is higher; negative means lower.</b> One decimal place is shown; values near zero indicate better alignment. This is an audio-based estimate, not a calibrated frequency counter.</p>
<p>Absolute offsets: below 5 Hz blue; 5–below 9 dark green; 9–below 11 yellow-green; 11–below 15 yellow; 15–below 20 orange; 20–below 25 vermilion. <b>C tuning ---</b> means no reliable value, for example no signal, insufficient MARK/SPACE identification, or an offset of at least 25 Hz. It does not guarantee successful decoding.</p>
<h3>Cross scope</h3><p>Use Cross scope beside Control to toggle its separate window. MARK is a horizontal ellipse and SPACE a vertical one. Thin, aligned ellipses approaching straight lines indicate tuning. Offsets tilt them; noise and tone transitions distort them. A sustained single tone shows only one ellipse. The display updates 5 times per second using short stable intervals, suppressing transitions. It retains the latest and previous trace in each direction, fading within about 0.7 seconds; the previous trace is dimmer. Up to four trails plus a dotted MARK/SPACE trajectory from the latest audio are displayed. Trails clear when tuning changes, input stops or the window is hidden. Frequencies follow the spectrum tones. Main-window width, connection and decoding are preserved. Closing with × or Esc also turns the button off.</p>
<p>This is an audio-based aid, not a calibrated instrument. Noise also produces traces. It is independent of SQ, the Decode checkbox and Display gain. Initially OFF.</p>
<h3>Waveform height and input level</h3><p>The <b>ochre horizontal line</b> slightly above the graph center is a display guide. Adjust Display gain so signal peaks reach or slightly exceed it. This makes peak positions easier to see. The line is neither a decoding test nor an SQ threshold.</p><p>The vertical scale uses a fixed reference with some averaging. Display gain affects only waveform height, not decoding or audio input level. Adjust input audio using Receive level in Audio settings.</p>'''),
('TX / Auto CQ', '''<h2>Macros and manual transmission</h2><p>Transmit with F1–F9 or the on-screen macro buttons. For manual TX, enter text and press TX. <b>STOP / Esc</b> stops transmission. Both upper-right and lower-right STOP buttons stop macros, manual TX and Auto CQ. Wait for audio to end and PTT to release before transmitting again. Interrupted transmissions are not automatically logged.</p>
<h3>Contest templates</h3><p>Blue instructions below F9 describe the selected template. Merely selecting a template does not change macros or SENT. Apply replaces the macros and sets SENT to its default: blank for normal QSOs, 01 for JARL WW, 25 for CQ WW; Fixed is enabled in every case. Edit SENT above Save, then Save to apply it to the main window.</p>
<table><tr><th>Template</th><th>SENT (Fixed ON)</th></tr><tr><td>JARL World Wide RTTY</td><td>Age at the start, or 01 (00 and 99 are also allowed). Multi-operator stations sending age use the average age.</td></tr><tr><td>CQ WW RTTY DX</td><td>Check the CQ Zone of your actual operating location and set it as your sent exchange. Japan is Zone 25.</td></tr></table>
<p>599 is already in the macro; do not include it in SENT. Leading zeros such as 01 are preserved. With the CQ WW template applied, RCVD also captures received state / province abbreviations (e.g. 05 MA). Check and correct the result.</p>
<h3>Auto CQ</h3><table><tr><th>Item</th><th>Behavior</th></tr><tr><td>Content</td><td>Repeats the F1 macro.</td></tr><tr><td>Count</td><td>Default 10; range 1–99.</td></tr><tr><td>Interval</td><td>Default 7 seconds after PTT release; range 1–99 seconds.</td></tr><tr><td>Input correction</td><td>Clamps above 99 to 99 and below 1 to 1. Blank / nonnumeric input returns to the default.</td></tr><tr><td>Stop</td><td>STOP, Esc or another macro.</td></tr></table>
<h3>Testing while disconnected</h3><p>Allow macros and manual TX while disconnected starts OFF. When ON, audio goes to Audio OUT without operating PTT. Even while disconnected, successful TU 73 / TU73 TX can log a QSO if auto log is ON and a contact CALL is present. If frequency is unavailable, ADIF FREQ and BAND are omitted. <b>Auto CQ always requires a radio connection.</b></p>'''),
('QSO / logs', '''<h2>Completing and saving a QSO</h2><p>If your transmitted text contains TU 73 or TU73, automatic logging is considered after successful TX. This applies to macros and manual TX, not received text. The old QSO complete checkbox is no longer needed. TU alone or 73 alone does not trigger logging.</p>
<table><tr><th>Auto log after sending TU 73</th><th>After successful TX</th></tr><tr><td>ON</td><td>Logs if a contact CALL is present. Uses the current time at TX completion in automatic mode, or the entered time in manual mode.</td></tr><tr><td>OFF</td><td>No automatic logging. Use Add to log.</td></tr></table>
<p>Stopped or failed TX is not auto-logged. Radio connection is not a requirement for auto logging. Changes to QSO fields or frequency during TX also prevent automatic logging.</p>
<p>Date (YYYY-MM-DD) and time (HH:MM) normally show the current time and cannot be edited. The colon alternates 2 seconds on / 2 seconds off. Manual date / time stops updates and enables editing; OFF returns to the current time. The date updates at midnight. Display uses the selected JST / UTC zone; ADIF is UTC. In manual mode, enter both date and time. With manual mode OFF, current time is used. Successful saving restores automatic time and clears contact CALL / RCVD; failure preserves the inputs.</p>
<p>During TX, received-text capture and automatic CALL / RST / exchange capture pause to prevent noise changing the fields. Manual editing or frequency changes skip automatic logging and explain why in the status and transcript. Automatic clock updates and blinking do not count as manual edits.</p>
<h3>Recent QSOs</h3><p>Double-click the table to open the QSO log. Wide windows use two columns; narrow windows use one. Numbers follow monthly ADIF filename order and record order within each file. Highest numbers appear top left, then top right, then the next row. Editing the time does not change record order. The lower-right frame shows the total log count. Deletion renumbers entries. View offers 2 / 4 / 6 / 8 / 10 entries (default 4), counting both columns together.</p>
<h3>Clear on frequency change</h3><p>Initially ON. Moving at least 20 Hz from the frequency at which CALL was captured or entered clears CALL, RST-R and RCVD. Previously saved preferences take priority.</p>
<h3>SENT</h3><p>Fixed starts ON. When OFF, successful logging increments numeric SENT (001 → 002). Nonnumeric and blank values remain unchanged. Normal QSOs may use blank SENT.</p>
<h2>Dates and logs</h2><p>On-screen time, manual input, recent QSOs and the QSO log use the selected JST / UTC zone; raw transcripts remain JST. The PC clock is used independently of its time zone. ADIF QSO time and monthly filenames use UTC. For example, October 1 at 00:30 JST is September 30 at 15:30 UTC, stored in 202609.adi. Manually entered past dates also use their UTC month. This month's ADIF opens the current UTC month's file; today's transcript uses the current JST date.</p>
<h3>Log files and backups</h3><p>ADIF is appended to YYYYMM.adi for the QSO UTC month. Raw TXT transcripts use YYYYMMDD_all.txt for the JST day. Menu labels specify UTC / JST and open the relevant file for the time of the click. Old logs are not automatically redistributed.</p>
<p>Edit → Backup settings offers backup on exit (initially ON) and after a selected number of QSOs (initially OFF, 30 QSOs). The counter counts newly saved QSOs and persists across restarts; edits and deletions do not increment it. Back up now works regardless of the checkboxes.</p>
<p>ADIF and raw TXT are saved together in a ZIP under logdata/backups, excluding previous backups. Success resets the counter to zero; failure preserves the counter and source logs and displays a notice. Save commits settings and Cancel discards changes. A manually created backup remains even after Cancel.</p>
<h3>Edit / delete log entries</h3><p><b>View → QSO log</b> shows saved ADIF entries. BAND combines wavelength and frequency, e.g. 15m / 21MHz. Select a row and Edit, or double-click, to change date, CALL, frequency, RST, SENT and RCVD. Delete asks for confirmation. The original ADIF is backed up under logdata/backups before changes; recent QSOs update too.</p>
<p>Normal-QSO QTH / INFO uses {MYQTH}, {MYJCCJCG} and {MYTXT}. Additional text can include your name and other details.</p>'''),
('Cabrillo export', '''<h2>ADIF export</h2><p><b>File → Export ADIF</b>: select start / end times (selected JST / UTC zone) and the operating callsign. Select at least one matching band and one QSO. Check the count and conditions, then save a separate ADIF file. Source logs are preserved.</p>
<h2>Cabrillo export</h2><p><b>File → Export Cabrillo</b> offers generic, JARL World Wide RTTY and CQ WW RTTY DX formats. For CQ WW, specify location (Japan / US and Canada / other), year, period (UTC) and submitting callsign. You cannot continue with no matching QSOs. Select at least one QSO and correct export-only values if needed. Mixed station callsigns produce a red warning but do not block proceeding.</p>
<p>Enter required and optional information, review the content and count, and save. CQ WW text uses ASCII letters, digits and symbols; romanize names and addresses. Check the station CQ Zone, read from SENT in the selected QSOs. LOCATION is DX outside the US and Canada; use a state / province abbreviation within them. Claimed score is optional and not calculated automatically. Empty optional CQ WW headers retain their field names.</p>
<p>Submit the saved Cabrillo file using the CQ WW RTTY official upload form displayed after saving. PSRTTY does not submit automatically. Output uses UTC, RY for RTTY, ASCII and CRLF. Source ADIF is unchanged. JARL export selects category, maximum power and operating location, then calculates provisional points: once per station per band, 2 points for the same continent and 3 for another. Manually assign operating locations for unidentified stations and cases such as JD1, then recalculate. Verify points and multipliers, optionally select a registered club number, and review the submission declaration.</p>
<p>The generic format allows a contest identifier and QSO field order. Check the recipient's requirements. Edited export values and selections do not modify source logs.</p>'''),
('Radio notes', '''<h2>ICOM</h2><p>Added in Ver0.77: IC-7200 / IC-7410 / IC-7600 / IC-9100 (documentation reviewed, hardware not tested). Select USB audio input and output separately. Set DATA modulation input to USB on the radio. IC-7600 uses D1, so set DATA1 input to USB. On IC-9100, select the operating MAIN side on the radio. Change filters on the radio for these four models.</p>
<p>Match the radio CI-V address. Manual settings are retained, including an IC-7300MK2 operated with the IC-7300 address.</p>
<h2>Yaesu / Kenwood (Hamlib)</h2><p>Supported selections include Yaesu FT-991/A, FTX-1, FT-710, FTDX10, FTDX101D/MP, FTDX3000 and Kenwood TS-590SG, TS-890S, TS-990S. About PSRTTY shows the Hamlib version and license. Specify CAT port and speed, then test the connection. For Yaesu, use Enhanced COM, USB DATA input and CAT RTS DISABLE (OFF). FTX-1 requires firmware supporting CAT.</p>
<h3>Mode selection</h3><p>Select the radio mode above Antenna TUNE in Control. ICOM offers LSB-D / USB-D / LSB / USB; Yaesu shows model-specific DATA modes; Kenwood shows LSB-DATA / USB-DATA / LSB / USB. TS-990S also offers D1–D3. Modes cannot change during TX. Automatic DATA selection runs only on connection; temporary changes here are not saved.</p>
<h3>Connection test</h3><p>A successful test connection ends after about 10 seconds. Press Connect for continued operation. If a test tone is transmitting, the test connection waits until transmission ends.</p>
<h3>Connection button</h3><p>Press Disconnected at the upper left to connect, or the blue Connect button to disconnect. Repeated clicks are blocked while connecting. Hover to see the model and COM port.</p>
<h3>Checking radio response</h3><p>Elapsed time alone does not cause disconnection. Three consecutive failed frequency responses disconnect the radio. A response in between preserves the connection. The reason and time appear in status, the connection tooltip and the operating transcript. Audio input continues.</p>
<h3>Startup and updating</h3><p>Startup checks log-folder access by creating, writing and deleting a temporary file. If creation or writing fails, the path and reason are shown and PSRTTY exits without opening the main window.</p><p>Only one instance can run from a given folder. Starting again brings the existing window to the front.</p>
<p><b>Help → Update PSRTTY</b> accepts a release ZIP, backs up settings and logs, updates and restarts. Developer PATCH ZIPs cannot be used. If versions are too far apart for in-app updating, close PSRTTY and extract the new binary release ZIP over the existing folder.</p>'''),
]

PAGES_EN.append(("Time / printer", '<h2>Time display (JST / UTC)</h2><p>View → Time display selects JST or UTC. Default JST; the selection is saved. Conversion uses the PC clock independently of its time zone. It applies to Current QSO, RX/TX cards, recent QSOs and the QSO log. Manual date/time keeps the same instant; invalid input prevents switching. ADIF remains UTC; raw TXT and HAMLOG CSV remain JST. Explicit time-zone labels in export dialogs still apply.</p><h2>RTTY printer</h2><p>File → RTTY printer offers Settings, Test print, Print content, Enable output and Clear queue. Output always starts OFF. Default content is Receive only (RX). Only main RX/TX is printed, excluding sub decoders. RX is queued when its card is finalized; TX is queued after successful transmission. Interrupted TX is not printed as a complete message.</p><p>The short-text filter starts ON at 1 character. It trims leading/trailing whitespace and counts only the body; display and logs are unchanged. Timestamps start ON and use the display zone at queue admission. Changing the zone later does not change queued jobs.</p><p>Select the USB serial COM port and speed (default 115200 bps, 8N1). Output is ASCII with LF, initially 32 columns and 0.5 seconds between lines. Use a separate COM port from radio/external control and explicitly select the radio COM port instead of Auto. Compatible firmware can use ACK mode, responding OK plus LF for each line. Normal mode cannot confirm physical printing: verify and adjust its delay on your hardware.</p><p>The default limit is 500 jobs. Queue-full or connection errors stop intake, preserve pending jobs and show status without dialogs, focus changes or sounds. Radio operation and logs continue. Enable output to resume. Uncertain partial jobs are never automatically retried. OFF, Clear queue and exit cancel unsent data; data already sent may still print.</p>'))

PAGES_EN[-1]=(PAGES_EN[-1][0], PAGES_EN[-1][1]+'<h2>Hide short received messages</h2><p>To the right of the Decode checkbox, select 0–10 characters. Default 0 disables filtering; 1 hides messages of one character or fewer. The complete body is checked at a newline or receive break, after trimming and collapsing whitespace. Hidden messages are not used for automatic extraction. Raw logs are preserved; printer filtering is independent. TX and sub-decoder displays are unchanged. The selection is saved.</p><h2>Printer status</h2><p>The menu switches between Enable and Disable printer output. Status is hidden while normally OFF. When enabled, it shows the selected content and queue count. Configuration problems, communication errors and queue limits remain visible without popups. Test printing and stopping also show status while jobs remain. The separator above Test print divides settings from actions.</p><h2>Special Thanks</h2><p>The button at the bottom of About PSRTTY opens a separate window thanking contributors to testing and development.</p>')

PAGES_EN.append(('HAMLOG / integrations', '<h2>HAMLOG and other integrations</h2>\n<h3>HAMLOG target: Ver5.48</h3><p>Uses the Turbo HAMLOG/Win public API introduced in Ver5.27c. On Windows, start one HAMLOG instance and open its QSO input window. Run both programs with the same privilege level.</p>\n<ol><li>Open <b>Integration → HAMLOG integration settings</b>. Check the connection; the responding window title is displayed.</li><li>Enable transfer when logging. This defaults to OFF. The default save mode fills HAMLOG input fields only; save the QSO in HAMLOG yourself. Alternatively, choose to request saving after transfer. Save these settings; they are shared by all profiles.</li><li>After PSRTTY saves a QSO to ADIF, it transfers it to HAMLOG. Manual logging and TU73 auto logging use the same path. PSRTTY increments the sent serial only once.</li></ol>\n<p>CALL, JST date/time, sent/received RST, frequency and RTTY mode are transferred. SENT and RCVD are retained in Remarks1; the station CALL is in Remarks2. Contest exchanges are not inferred as JCC/JCG codes. HAMLOG QSL, Code, GL, Name and QTH settings/lookup results are retained.</p>\n<h3>CALL / Name / QTH lookup</h3><p>Lookup in the settings window enters a CALL in HAMLOG’s empty input window, invokes HAMLOG’s Enter processing and displays Name/QTH. Results depend on HAMLOG lookup settings and stored records and may be empty. Logging that same CALL fills the draft created by lookup. If the draft was edited in HAMLOG, automatic transfer is stopped to protect it. Save or clear the draft before proceeding.</p>\n<h3>Saving and transfer failures</h3><p>In input-only mode, save the QSO in HAMLOG before recording the next QSO. Other QSO drafts are never overwritten. Automatic saving reads back and checks the transfer before invoking HAMLOG’s Save API. You can check the result in HAMLOG’s input window and log.</p><p>Failures show a dialog with the reason and suggested action. The PSRTTY ADIF record is already saved: do not add that QSO again. Interrupted transfers are journaled and not retried automatically. Check HAMLOG and manually enter only missing QSOs, or use File → HAMLOG CSV export. Records, edits and deletions in HAMLOG do not synchronize back to PSRTTY.</p>\n<h3>zLog target: 3.0.4.0 (ZLOG3040)</h3><p>Integration → zLog Reiwa integration settings opens ADIF export. Selected QSOs are transferred by file, without live radio/log control. File → ADIF export opens the same export wizard.</p><ol><li>In zLog, select the matching contest, station CALL and time settings. ADIF times are UTC; zLog converts them according to its contest settings.</li><li>Select the export format, period, station CALL, bands and QSOs, then save.</li><li>Import the ADIF file in zLog and check count, time, band, mode, both RSTs, both exchanges and multipliers. Avoid importing the same file twice.</li></ol>\n<table><tr><th>Format</th><th>Exchange mapping</th></tr><tr><td>Standard ADIF</td><td>STX_STRING/SRX_STRING and numeric STX/SRX, as before.</td></tr><tr><td>General zLog</td><td>Also retains original SENT/RCVD and station CALL in COMMENT.</td></tr><tr><td>JARL WW RTTY</td><td>Received age also goes into AGE. Enter one or two digits.</td></tr><tr><td>CQ WW RTTY</td><td>Received zone goes into CQZ and state/province into STATE, e.g. 05 MA. Zone must be 01–40.</td></tr></table>\n<p><b>The target zLog ADIF importer does not include state/province in the CQ WW RTTY received exchange.</b>After import, use the original RCVD in the note to correct affected exchanges and multipliers. Numeric fields may lose leading zeroes in their display; the original text is retained in string fields and notes. Check contest configuration and exchanges before submitting a contest log.</p>\n<h3>Radio controls</h3><p>NB and NR read/write are enabled through Hamlib for all listed Yaesu/Kenwood models, with state readback. Yaesu uses the DNR label. FT-991/FT-991A, FTX-1, FT-710, FTDX10, FTDX101D/MP and FTDX3000 offer mode-specific receive widths. TS-590SG DATA/SSB width is adjusted by changing high cut while preserving low cut.</p><p>Hamlib 4.7.2 does not implement receive bandwidth control for TS-890S/TS-990S; use the radio controls for width. NB/NR remain available. Controls are disabled during TX or when connection/state retrieval fails.</p>'))

PAGES_EN[0] = (PAGES_EN[0][0], PAGES_EN[0][1] + '\n<h3>IC-7760 (Ver1.08)</h3><p>Default CI-V address: B2. Select the USB CI-V COM port and set DATA1 modulation input to USB on the radio. LSB-D/USB-D selects DATA1. Controls apply to the selected receiver; check the transmit side on the radio.</p>\n<h3>Secondary Audio OUT</h3><p>Open Secondary Audio OUT settings to the left of Save on the Audio OUT tab. Confirm and save the primary device and volume before opening. Cancel or save failure leaves the window closed. The primary device, including Auto, is excluded from the secondary list. Enable it (default OFF), select a different device from the primary output, choose volume and Save. Close discards unsaved changes. Settings are stored per profile.</p><p>The same TX waveform is sent for monitoring or other uses, with independent volume from 0–200% (default 100%). Above 100% the slider turns red and audio may distort. This window has no PTT or test controls. Stopping TX also stops the secondary output. While disconnected, enabling both disconnected TX and secondary Audio OUT allows playback through either available output. If neither works, a reason is displayed. Connected radio transmission still requires the primary output. On errors, check the selected device and connection as indicated in the status area.</p>')

